{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05364","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05364","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05364","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexander M.","familyName":"Fichtl","name":"Fichtl, Alexander M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jeremias","familyName":"Bohn","name":"Bohn, Jeremias","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Josefin","familyName":"Kelber","name":"Kelber, Josefin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Edoardo","familyName":"Mosca","name":"Mosca, Edoardo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Georg","familyName":"Groh","name":"Groh, Georg","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The End of Transformers? On Challenging Attention and the Rise of Sub-Quadratic Architectures"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T20:45:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-02T01:19:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.18653/v1/2026.bigpicture-main.6","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Transformers have dominated sequence processing tasks for the past seven years -- most notably language modeling. However, the inherent quadratic complexity of their attention mechanism remains a significant bottleneck as context length increases. This paper surveys recent efforts to overcome this bottleneck, including advances in (sub-quadratic) attention variants, recurrent neural networks, state space models, and hybrid architectures. We critically analyze these approaches in terms of compute and memory complexity, benchmark results, and fundamental limitations to assess whether the dominance of pure-attention transformers may soon be challenged."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"21 pages, 2 figures, 2 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05364","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-08T01:47:13Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:47:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-02T02:59:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04533","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04533","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04533","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyunmin","familyName":"Cho","name":"Cho, Hyunmin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Donghoon","familyName":"Ahn","name":"Ahn, Donghoon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Susung","familyName":"Hong","name":"Hong, Susung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jee Eun","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Jee Eun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seungryong","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Seungryong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kyong Hwan","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Kyong Hwan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TAG: Tangential Amplifying Guidance for Hallucination-Resistant Sampling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T06:53:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:17:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-26T13:18:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-27T00:59:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diffusion models achieve state-of-the-art image generation but often produce semantic inconsistencies, or hallucinations. Existing inference-time guidance methods rely on external signals or architectural modifications, adding computational overhead. We propose $\\mathbf{T}$angential $\\mathbf{A}$mplifying $\\mathbf{G}$uidance $\\mathbf{(TAG)}$, a training-free, architecture-agnostic, plug-and-play guidance method that operates purely on trajectory signals. TAG uses an intermediate sample as a projection basis and amplifies the tangential components of the estimated score to correct the sampling trajectory. A first-order Taylor analysis shows that this steers the state toward higher-probability regions of the data manifold, reducing inconsistencies and improving fidelity while adding negligible overhead to existing samplers. Code is available at our Project Page (https://hyeon-cho.github.io/TAG/)."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to ICML 2026 (Regular)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04533","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:16:50Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:16:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-27T02:36:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.08592","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.08592","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.08592","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shahriar Kabir","familyName":"Nahin","name":"Nahin, Shahriar Kabir","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hadi","familyName":"Askari","name":"Askari, Hadi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Muhao","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Muhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anshuman","familyName":"Chhabra","name":"Chhabra, Anshuman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Less Diverse, Less Safe: The Indirect But Pervasive Risk of Test-Time Scaling in Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T20:01:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-13T00:00:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-29T03:34:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-30T01:20:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-09T15:18:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-12T00:49:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Test-Time Scaling (TTS) improves LLM reasoning by exploring multiple candidate responses and then operating over this set to find the best output. A tacit premise behind TTS is that sufficiently diverse candidate pools enhance reliability. In this work, we show that this assumption in TTS introduces a previously unrecognized failure mode. When candidate diversity is curtailed, even by a modest amount, TTS becomes much more likely to produce unsafe outputs. We present a reference-guided diversity reduction protocol (RefDiv) that serves as a diagnostic attack to stress test TTS pipelines. Through extensive experiments across open-source models (e.g. Qwen3, Mistral, Llama3.1, Gemma3) and two widely used TTS strategies (Monte Carlo Tree Search and Best-of-N), constraining diversity consistently signifies the rate at which TTS produces unsafe results. The effect is often stronger than that produced by prompts directly with high adversarial intent scores. This observed phenomenon also transfers across TTS strategies and to closed-source models (e.g. OpenAI o3-mini and Gemini-2.5-Pro), thus indicating that this is a general and extant property of TTS rather than a model-specific artifact. Additionally, we find that numerous widely used safety guardrail classifiers (e.g. Llama-Guard), are unable to flag the adversarial input prompts generated by RefDiv, demonstrating that existing defenses offer limited protection against this diversity-driven failure mode."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to ICML 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.08592","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-13T01:40:00Z","registered":"2025-10-13T01:40:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-12T05:04:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04428","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04428","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04428","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuanhao","familyName":"Zou","name":"Zou, Yuanhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shengji","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Shengji","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andong","familyName":"Deng","name":"Deng, Andong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Youpeng","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Youpeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A.I.R.: Enabling Adaptive, Iterative, and Reasoning-based Frame Selection For Video Question Answering"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T01:51:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:10:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:08:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-27T01:13:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-27T02:48:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-30T00:20:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Effectively applying Vision-Language Models (VLMs) to Video Question Answering (VideoQA) hinges on selecting a concise yet comprehensive set of frames, as processing entire videos is computationally infeasible. However, current frame selection methods face a critical trade-off: approaches relying on lightweight similarity models, such as CLIP, often fail to capture the nuances of complex queries, resulting in inaccurate similarity scores that cannot reflect the authentic query-frame relevance, which further undermines frame selection. Meanwhile, methods that leverage a VLM for deeper analysis achieve higher accuracy but incur prohibitive computational costs. To address these limitations, we propose A.I.R., a training-free approach for Adaptive, Iterative, and Reasoning-based frame selection. We leverage a powerful VLM to perform deep, semantic analysis on complex queries, and this analysis is deployed within a cost-effective iterative loop that processes only a small batch of the most high-potential frames at a time. Extensive experiments on various VideoQA benchmarks demonstrate that our approach outperforms existing frame selection methods, significantly boosts the performance of the foundation VLM, and achieves substantial gains in computational efficiency over other VLM-based techniques."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICLR 2026 Paper"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04428","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:14:15Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:14:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-30T02:11:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04633","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04633","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04633","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lukas","familyName":"Gienapp","name":"Gienapp, Lukas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Martin","familyName":"Potthast","name":"Potthast, Martin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrew","familyName":"Yates","name":"Yates, Andrew","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Harrisen","familyName":"Scells","name":"Scells, Harrisen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eugene","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Eugene","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Topic-Specific Classifiers are Better Relevance Judges than Prompted LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T09:38:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:22:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-23T14:11:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-26T01:39:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The unjudged document problem, where systems that did not contribute to the original judgement pool may retrieve documents without a relevance judgement, is a key obstacle to the reuseability of test collections in information retrieval. While the de facto standard to deal with the problem is to treat unjudged documents as non-relevant, many alternatives have been proposed, such as the use of large language models (LLMs) as a relevance judge (LLM-as-a-judge). However, this has been criticized, among other things, as circular, since the same LLM can be used as the ranker and the judge. We propose to train topic-specific relevance classifiers instead: By finetuning monoT5 with independent LoRA weight adaptation on the judgments of a single assessor for a single topic's pool, we align it to that assessor's notion of relevance for the topic. The system rankings obtained through our classifier's relevance judgments achieve a Spearmans' $ρ$ correlation of $\u0026gt;0.94$ with ground truth system rankings. As little as 128 initial human judgments per topic suffice to improve the comparability of models, compared to treating unjudged documents as non-relevant, while achieving more reliability than existing LLM-as-a-judge approaches. Topic-specific relevance classifiers are thus a lightweight and straightforward way to tackle the unjudged document problem, while maintaining human judgments as the gold standard for retrieval evaluation. Code, models, and data are made openly available."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages, 3 figures, 4 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04633","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:19:15Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:19:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-26T02:49:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04564","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04564","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04564","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Honglin","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Honglin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chao","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Chao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peng","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Peng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunfan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yunfan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xi","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Xi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Conditional Representation Learning for Customized Tasks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T08:00:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:19:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-13T08:39:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-16T01:26:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Conventional representation learning methods learn a universal representation that primarily captures dominant semantics, which may not always align with customized downstream tasks. For instance, in animal habitat analysis, researchers prioritize scene-related features, whereas universal embeddings emphasize categorical semantics, leading to suboptimal results. As a solution, existing approaches resort to supervised fine-tuning, which however incurs high computational and annotation costs. In this paper, we propose Conditional Representation Learning (CRL), aiming to extract representations tailored to arbitrary user-specified criteria. Specifically, we reveal that the semantics of a space are determined by its basis, thereby enabling a set of descriptive words to approximate the basis for a customized feature space. Building upon this insight, given a user-specified criterion, CRL first employs a large language model (LLM) to generate descriptive texts to construct the semantic basis, then projects the image representation into this conditional feature space leveraging a vision-language model (VLM). The conditional representation better captures semantics for the specific criterion, which could be utilized for multiple customized tasks. Extensive experiments on classification and retrieval tasks demonstrate the superiority and generality of the proposed CRL. The code is available at https://github.com/XLearning-SCU/2025-NeurIPS-CRL."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04564","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:17:35Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:17:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-16T04:50:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04539","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04539","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04539","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zeng","familyName":"Tao","name":"Tao, Zeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheng","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Zheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zeyuan","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Zeyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Leizhi","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Leizhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhuowen","familyName":"Tu","name":"Tu, Zhuowen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"C3Editor: Achieving Controllable Consistency in 2D Model for 3D Editing"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Graphics (cs.GR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T07:07:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:17:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-31T16:06:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-03T01:52:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Existing 2D-lifting-based 3D editing methods often encounter challenges related to inconsistency, stemming from the lack of view-consistent 2D editing models and the difficulty of ensuring consistent editing across multiple views. To address these issues, we propose C3Editor, a controllable and consistent 2D-lifting-based 3D editing framework. Given an original 3D representation and a text-based editing prompt, our method selectively establishes a view-consistent 2D editing model to achieve superior 3D editing results. The process begins with the controlled selection of a ground truth (GT) view and its corresponding edited image as the optimization target, allowing for user-defined manual edits. Next, we fine-tune the 2D editing model within the GT view and across multiple views to align with the GT-edited image while ensuring multi-view consistency. To meet the distinct requirements of GT view fitting and multi-view consistency, we introduce separate LoRA modules for targeted fine-tuning. Our approach delivers more consistent and controllable 2D and 3D editing results than existing 2D-lifting-based methods, outperforming them in both qualitative and quantitative evaluations."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICCV 2025 Workshop Wild3D"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04539","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:16:59Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:17:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-03T03:01:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04483","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04483","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04483","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zechao","familyName":"Zhan","name":"Zhan, Zechao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weixin","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Weixin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziwei","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Ziwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xubin","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xubin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tiezheng","familyName":"Ge","name":"Ge, Tiezheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TBStar-Edit: From Image Editing Pattern Shifting to Consistency Enhancement"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T04:46:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:14:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-10T06:02:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-13T00:25:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-15T14:07:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-16T00:52:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in image generation and editing technologies have enabled state-of-the-art models to achieve impressive results in general domains. However, when applied to e-commerce scenarios, these general models often encounter consistency limitations. To address this challenge, we introduce TBStar-Edit, an new image editing model tailored for the e-commerce domain. Through rigorous data engineering, model architecture design and training strategy, TBStar-Edit achieves precise and high-fidelity image editing while maintaining the integrity of product appearance and layout. Specifically, for data engineering, we establish a comprehensive data construction pipeline, encompassing data collection, construction, filtering, and augmentation, to acquire high-quality, instruction-following, and strongly consistent editing data to support model training. For model architecture design, we design a hierarchical model framework consisting of a base model, pattern shifting modules, and consistency enhancement modules. For model training, we adopt a two-stage training strategy to enhance the consistency preservation: first stage for editing pattern shifting, and second stage for consistency enhancement. Each stage involves training different modules with separate datasets. Finally, we conduct extensive evaluations of TBStar-Edit on a self-proposed e-commerce benchmark, and the results demonstrate that TBStar-Edit outperforms existing general-domain editing models in both objective metrics (VIE Score) and subjective user preference."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04483","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:15:37Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:15:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-16T02:26:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04587","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04587","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04587","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sheng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Sheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruiming","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Ruiming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Charles","familyName":"Herndon","name":"Herndon, Charles","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yihang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yihang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shunsuke","familyName":"Koga","name":"Koga, Shunsuke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jeanne","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Jeanne","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhi","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Zhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Pathology-CoT: Learning Visual Chain-of-Thought Agent from Expert Whole Slide Image Diagnosis Behavior"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T08:44:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:20:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-13T18:37:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-15T00:03:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diagnosing a whole-slide image is an interactive, multi-stage process of changing magnification and moving between fields. Although recent pathology foundation models demonstrated superior performances, practical agentic systems that decide what field to examine next, adjust magnification, and deliver explainable diagnoses are still lacking. Such limitation is largely bottlenecked by data: scalable, clinically aligned supervision of expert viewing behavior that is tacit and experience-based, not documented in textbooks or internet, and therefore absent from LLM training. Here we introduce a framework designed to address this challenge through three key breakthroughs. First, the AI Session Recorder seamlessly integrates with standard whole-slide image viewers to unobtrusively record routine navigation and convert the viewer logs into standardized behavioral commands and bounding boxes. Second, a lightweight human-in-the-loop review turns AI-drafted rationales for behavioral commands into the Pathology-CoT dataset, a form of paired \"where to look\" and \"why it matters\", enabling six-fold faster labeling compared to manual constructing such Chain-of-Thought dataset. Using this behavioral data, we build Pathology-o3, a two-stage agent that first proposes important ROIs and then performs behavior-guided reasoning. On the gastrointestinal lymph-node metastasis detection task, our method achieved 100 recall on the internal validation from Stanford Medicine and 97.6 recall on an independent external validation from Sweden, exceeding the state-of-the-art OpenAI o3 model and generalizing across backbones. To our knowledge, Pathology-CoT constitutes one of the first behavior-grounded agentic systems in pathology. Turning everyday viewer logs into scalable, expert-validated supervision, our framework makes agentic pathology practical and establishes a path to human-aligned, upgradeable clinical AI."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04587","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:18:08Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:18:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-15T02:22:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.08595","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.08595","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.08595","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"V. S. Raghu","familyName":"Parupudi","name":"Parupudi, V. S. Raghu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Systematic Diagnosis of Brittle Reasoning in Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T21:40:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-13T00:00:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"A central question in artificial intelligence is the extent to which machine learning models comprehend mathematics. To address this, we propose a novel framework for measuring mathematical reasoning that moves beyond standard benchmarks to diagnose specific failure points. Our method first generates structured, step-by-step reasoning from gpt-3.5-turbo on the GSM8K dataset. We then use a more capable analyst model, gpt-4o-mini, to categorize errors and, crucially, perform an unsupervised clustering of every reasoning sentence to identify emergent \"reasoning modes.\" This analysis reveals a cognitive profile with a stark, nonhuman-like brittleness: while the model achieves near-perfect accuracy on procedural modes like sequential calculation, its performance on modes requiring combinatorial reasoning with restrictions plummets. By identifying and quantifying the reliability of these distinct reasoning skills, our work provides a more granular method to evaluate mathematical comprehension and offers a precise roadmap for developing new capabilities and more reliable future applications."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Submitted to NEURIPS-2025 MATHAI workshop"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.08595","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-13T01:40:05Z","registered":"2025-10-13T01:40:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-13T01:40:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05396","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05396","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05396","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nilesh","familyName":"Gupta","name":"Gupta, Nilesh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chong","familyName":"You","name":"You, Chong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Srinadh","familyName":"Bhojanapalli","name":"Bhojanapalli, Srinadh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sanjiv","familyName":"Kumar","name":"Kumar, Sanjiv","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Inderjit","familyName":"Dhillon","name":"Dhillon, Inderjit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Felix","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Felix","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Scalable In-context Ranking with Generative Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T21:41:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:11:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T02:02:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:18:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In-context Ranking (ICR) is an emerging paradigm for Information Retrieval (IR), which leverages contextual understanding of LLMs by directly incorporating the task description, candidate documents, and the query into the model's input prompt and tasking the LLM to identify relevant document(s). While it is effective, efficiency is a significant challenge in this paradigm, especially as the candidate list grows due to quadratic/super-linear scaling of attention operation with context length. To this end, this paper first identifies inherent and exploitable structures in the attention of LLMs finetuned for ICR: (1) inter-document block sparsity: attention is dense within each document block but sparse across different documents in the context; and (2) query-document block relevance: the attention scores from certain query tokens to a document block in middle layers strongly correlate with that document's actual relevance. Motivated by these observations, we introduce BlockRank (Blockwise In-context Ranking), a novel method that adapts the attention operation in an LLM by (a) architecturally enforcing the observed inter-document block sparsity, reducing attention complexity from quadratic to linear without loss in performance, and (b) optimizing query-document block relevance for true relevant documents during fine-tuning using an auxiliary contrastive training objective, improving retrieval in attention. Experiments on BEIR, MSMarco and NQ with Mistral-7B demonstrate that BlockRank Mistral matches or outperforms existing SOTA listwise rankers and controlled fine-tuned baseline while being significantly more efficient at inference (4.7x for 100 MSMarco documents in context) and scaling gracefully to long-context shortlists, around 500 documents in-context (approximately 100K context length) within a second, presenting a scalable and effective solution for ICR."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05396","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:47:56Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:47:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T02:22:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05288","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05288","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05288","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruoxing","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Ruoxing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DP-Adam-AC: Privacy-preserving Fine-Tuning of Localizable Language Models Using Adam Optimization with Adaptive Clipping"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T18:56:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:06:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) such as ChatGPT have evolved into powerful and ubiquitous tools. Fine-tuning on small datasets allows LLMs to acquire specialized skills for specific tasks efficiently. Although LLMs provide great utility in both general and task-specific use cases, they are limited by two security-related concerns. First, traditional LLM hardware requirements make them infeasible to run locally on consumer-grade devices. A remote network connection with the LLM provider's server is usually required, making the system vulnerable to network attacks. Second, fine-tuning an LLM for a sensitive task may involve sensitive data. Non-private fine-tuning algorithms produce models vulnerable to training data reproduction attacks. Our work addresses these security concerns by enhancing differentially private optimization algorithms and applying them to fine-tune localizable language models. We introduce adaptable gradient clipping along with other engineering enhancements to the standard DP-Adam optimizer to create DP-Adam-AC. We use our optimizer to fine-tune examples of two localizable LLM designs, small language model (Qwen2.5-0.5B) and 1.58 bit quantization (Bitnet-b1.58-2B). We demonstrate promising improvements in loss through experimentation with two synthetic datasets."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05288","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:45:28Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:45:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:45:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04637","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04637","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04637","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zeyi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zeyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanju","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Yanju","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Heyuan","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Heyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tenglong","familyName":"Ao","name":"Ao, Tenglong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaohang","familyName":"Zhan","name":"Zhan, Xiaohang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Libin","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Libin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Social Agent: Mastering Dyadic Nonverbal Behavior Generation via Conversational LLM Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Graphics (cs.GR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T09:41:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:23:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3757377.3763879","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present Social Agent, a novel framework for synthesizing realistic and contextually appropriate co-speech nonverbal behaviors in dyadic conversations. In this framework, we develop an agentic system driven by a Large Language Model (LLM) to direct the conversation flow and determine appropriate interactive behaviors for both participants. Additionally, we propose a novel dual-person gesture generation model based on an auto-regressive diffusion model, which synthesizes coordinated motions from speech signals. The output of the agentic system is translated into high-level guidance for the gesture generator, resulting in realistic movement at both the behavioral and motion levels. Furthermore, the agentic system periodically examines the movements of interlocutors and infers their intentions, forming a continuous feedback loop that enables dynamic and responsive interactions between the two participants. User studies and quantitative evaluations show that our model significantly improves the quality of dyadic interactions, producing natural, synchronized nonverbal behaviors."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"SIGGRAPH ASIA 2025 (Conference Track); Project page: https://pku-mocca.github.io/Social-Agent-Page/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04637","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-07T03:19:21Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:19:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:19:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04576","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04576","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04576","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuhta","familyName":"Takida","name":"Takida, Yuhta","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Satoshi","familyName":"Hayakawa","name":"Hayakawa, Satoshi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Takashi","familyName":"Shibuya","name":"Shibuya, Takashi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Masaaki","familyName":"Imaizumi","name":"Imaizumi, Masaaki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Naoki","familyName":"Murata","name":"Murata, Naoki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bac","familyName":"Nguyen","name":"Nguyen, Bac","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Toshimitsu","familyName":"Uesaka","name":"Uesaka, Toshimitsu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chieh-Hsin","familyName":"Lai","name":"Lai, Chieh-Hsin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuki","familyName":"Mitsufuji","name":"Mitsufuji, Yuki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SONA: Learning Conditional, Unconditional, and Mismatching-Aware Discriminator"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T08:26:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:19:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Deep generative models have made significant advances in generating complex content, yet conditional generation remains a fundamental challenge. Existing conditional generative adversarial networks often struggle to balance the dual objectives of assessing authenticity and conditional alignment of input samples within their conditional discriminators. To address this, we propose a novel discriminator design that integrates three key capabilities: unconditional discrimination, matching-aware supervision to enhance alignment sensitivity, and adaptive weighting to dynamically balance all objectives. Specifically, we introduce Sum of Naturalness and Alignment (SONA), which employs separate projections for naturalness (authenticity) and alignment in the final layer with an inductive bias, supported by dedicated objective functions and an adaptive weighting mechanism. Extensive experiments on class-conditional generation tasks show that \\ours achieves superior sample quality and conditional alignment compared to state-of-the-art methods. Furthermore, we demonstrate its effectiveness in text-to-image generation, confirming the versatility and robustness of our approach."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"24 pages with 9 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04576","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:17:53Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:17:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:17:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04450","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04450","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04450","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiyuan","familyName":"He","name":"He, Qiyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yicong","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yicong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haotian","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Haotian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinghao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jinghao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyao","familyName":"Liao","name":"Liao, Xinyao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pheng-Ann","familyName":"Heng","name":"Heng, Pheng-Ann","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stefano","familyName":"Ermon","name":"Ermon, Stefano","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"James","familyName":"Zou","name":"Zou, James","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Angela","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Angela","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"REAR: Rethinking Visual Autoregressive Models via Generator-Tokenizer Consistency Regularization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T02:48:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:12:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Visual autoregressive (AR) generation offers a promising path toward unifying vision and language models, yet its performance remains suboptimal against diffusion models. Prior work often attributes this gap to tokenizer limitations and rasterization ordering. In this work, we identify a core bottleneck from the perspective of generator-tokenizer inconsistency, i.e., the AR-generated tokens may not be well-decoded by the tokenizer. To address this, we propose reAR, a simple training strategy introducing a token-wise regularization objective: when predicting the next token, the causal transformer is also trained to recover the visual embedding of the current token and predict the embedding of the target token under a noisy context. It requires no changes to the tokenizer, generation order, inference pipeline, or external models. Despite its simplicity, reAR substantially improves performance. On ImageNet, it reduces gFID from 3.02 to 1.86 and improves IS to 316.9 using a standard rasterization-based tokenizer. When applied to advanced tokenizers, it achieves a gFID of 1.42 with only 177M parameters, matching the performance with larger state-of-the-art diffusion models (675M)."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"27 pages, 23 figures, 5 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04450","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:14:49Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:14:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:14:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04417","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04417","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04417","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenyuan","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Wenyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adithya","familyName":"Balachandran","name":"Balachandran, Adithya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chao","familyName":"Tian","name":"Tian, Chao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Paul Pu","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Paul Pu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Partial Information Decomposition via Normalizing Flows in Latent Gaussian Distributions"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Theory (cs.IT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T01:08:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:10:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The study of multimodality has garnered significant interest in fields where the analysis of interactions among multiple information sources can enhance predictive modeling, data fusion, and interpretability. Partial information decomposition (PID) has emerged as a useful information-theoretic framework to quantify the degree to which individual modalities independently, redundantly, or synergistically convey information about a target variable. However, existing PID methods depend on optimizing over a joint distribution constrained by estimated pairwise probability distributions, which are costly and inaccurate for continuous and high-dimensional modalities. Our first key insight is that the problem can be solved efficiently when the pairwise distributions are multivariate Gaussians, and we refer to this problem as Gaussian PID (GPID). We propose a new gradient-based algorithm that substantially improves the computational efficiency of GPID based on an alternative formulation of the underlying optimization problem. To generalize the applicability to non-Gaussian data, we learn information-preserving encoders to transform random variables of arbitrary input distributions into pairwise Gaussian random variables. Along the way, we resolved an open problem regarding the optimality of joint Gaussian solutions for GPID. Empirical validation in diverse synthetic examples demonstrates that our proposed method provides more accurate and efficient PID estimates than existing baselines. We further evaluate a series of large-scale multimodal benchmarks to show its utility in real-world applications of quantifying PID in multimodal datasets and selecting high-performing models."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"NeurIPS 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04417","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:13:59Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:14:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:14:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":16,"totalPages":1,"page":1},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=100\u0026query=%28doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.08592+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.08595+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04417+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04428+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04450+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04483+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04533+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04539+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04564+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04576+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04587+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04633+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04637+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.05288+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.05364+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.05396%29"}}